    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2024857946241618265",
      "url": "https://x.com/Ecoflowing/status/2024857946241618265",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/Ecoflowing/status/2024857946241618265",
      "text": "🧵 Lagos Is a Coastal Megacity. Waste Is Not Just a Sanitation Issue — It’s a Survival Issue.\n\n1.\nLagos is not just a big city.\n\nIt is a coastal megacity with over 20 million people, sitting on fragile lagoons, creeks and the Atlantic shoreline.\n\nThat changes everything about waste.\n\n2.\nIn inland cities, mismanaged waste creates land problems.\n\nIn coastal cities, mismanaged waste creates water problems.\n\nAnd water problems multiply faster.\n\n3.\nWhen plastic blocks drainage in Lagos, it is not cosmetic.\n\nFlood risk rises.\nStagnant water increases disease.\nMarine ecosystems absorb the consequences.\n\nWaste does not stay where it is dumped.\n\nIt travels.\n\n4.\nEvery rainfall becomes a stress test.\n\nCan the drainage system breathe?\nCan canals flow freely?\nCan the lagoon absorb runoff without choking?\n\nWaste determines that answer.\n\n5. \nIn a coastal city, poor waste management is directly linked to:\n\nUrban flooding\nPublic health risk\nMarine pollution\nEconomic disruption\nInfrastructure decay\n\nThis is not aesthetics. It is resilience.\n\n6.\nLagos markets generate massive organic waste daily.\n\nIf unmanaged, it decomposes into methane.\n\nIf diverted properly, it becomes compost or energy.\n\nA coastal megacity cannot afford to treat organic waste casually.\n\n7.\nPlastic in Lagos does not just sit on roads.\n\nIt enters the lagoon.\nIt reaches the Atlantic.\nIt affects fisheries.\nIt affects tourism.\n\nMarine pollution becomes international visibility.\n\n8.\nThe population density amplifies everything.\n\nMillions generating daily waste in tight urban corridors means logistics must be precise.\n\nIf collection lags even slightly, accumulation accelerates quickly.\n\nCoastal cities have less margin for error.\n\n9.\nWaste management in Lagos is therefore not only an environmental matter.\n\nIt is:\n\nClimate adaptation\nUrban planning\nPublic health strategy\nEconomic protection\n\n10.\nMany global coastal cities have learned this lesson the hard way.\n\nJakarta.\nManila.\nMumbai.\n\nDrainage failure and waste mismanagement compound climate risk.\n\nLagos cannot afford delay.\n\n11.\nThe conversation must move beyond “keep the city clean.”\n\nThe real conversation is:\n\nHow does a dense coastal megacity build a waste system strong enough to protect its future?\n\n12.\nInland cities can recover slowly from poor waste control.\n\nCoastal megacities pay faster.\n\nWater does not negotiate.",
